26582	26762	26567	26567	he	413	“ And I indeed am not to be pierced by the shafts of Fortune . My armour is the _ aes triplex _ of a clear conscience , and a mind nourished by the precepts of philosophy . ‘ For men , ’ says Epictetus , ‘ are disturbed not by things themselves , but by their opinions or thoughts concerning those things . ’ And again , ‘ whosoever will be free , let him not desire or dread that which it is in the power of others either to deny or inflict : otherwise , he is a slave . ’ And of all such gifts as are dependent on the caprice of fortune or of men , I have long ago learned to say , with Horace — who , however , is too wavering in his philosophy , vacillating between the precepts of Zeno and the less worthy maxims of Epicurus , and attempting , as we say , ‘ duabus sellis sedere’—concerning such accidents , I say , with the pregnant brevity of the poet —
